{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner correction before independent review of R1, exact head 8416c414df68f11b2a789ebb686de0bf1895c4d4, delivered in 4131c1f4. Full 842-line contract and full 420-line admission-read inventory read; two-file design-only diff passes git diff --check. No runtime/code/probe was run. This is internal planning correction, not independent approval or protocol adoption. Whole-head validation per scope is a sound conservative direction once every relevant local dependency is actually covered; naming R3/R4 gaps is appropriate, not a completed freshness/publication proof. Please correct these seven groups and reconcile R0 correction b9eb7419 before independent filing.\n\n1. Remove hash and signature fixed points; specify construction order and concrete byte domains. CreationRecord has opening:SignedIntent, but section 4's SignedIntent already names ScopeRef containing the digest of that CreationRecord AND an incarnation minted only after writing storage. The root as well as children cannot construct that signature as written. Child CreationRecord.parent is the creator entry's FactRef, including hash, while that same creator entry contains the full CreationRecord as its create duty; hashing that entry therefore needs its own hash first. RelationshipMessage.owner and ref-effect \"this entry\" create the same problem if expanded to this entry's hash inside its bytes. A retry-stable seed/creation-intent domain and a post-seal reference/receipt derivation or an earlier source fact can avoid it; choose explicitly. Show root creation, one child creation and one relationship update in exact build/sign/seal/deliver order, which bytes include which hashes, when incarnation is known, and which post-seal references are views rather than recursively embedded payloads. Preserve source-owned intent/causal proof, no client preallocation from prospective seq, and retry-stable identity. Section 2 also says DO lookup is by scope ID alone then by kind+ID: pick one canonical namespace/route rule that cannot expose duplicate ordering authorities for one identity. Restore from retained bytes must keep the same incarnation; data loss without proof of prior absence must be conflict/unavailable, not silently remint a functioning replacement that issues fresh grants or effects.\n\n2. Keep every time dependency sound at commit, including indirect hold and timed-transition predicates. Expiring due transitions only before Snapshot is insufficient when an awaited preparation crosses a deadline. A guard such as \"some held\" or \"none held\", or a hold renewal/handover effect, depends on time even if its written form only reads a state. Before commit, detect/apply newly due transitions (and then restart on the changed head) or derive their equivalent guarded semantics consistently. Use one recorded commit-time input for expiry, freshness and derived deadlines; rederive time-dependent effects too, not just explicit before/after guards. Define backward-clock behavior separately from wall-clock freshness: monotonic clamping is not proof that an expiring credential remains currently valid. Do not describe all values before Snapshot as forbidden when immutable data/signature checks and configured budgets can safely preexist; classify the dependency. Every writer of authoritative local grant, eligibility, receipt and duty state must use the same commit protocol or a separately stated fence/recheck. Pure caches/bookkeeping may stay outside history only if no semantic guard reads their unrecorded mutable state. Keep bounded retries/timeouts and unavailable results so a slow foreign-fact read does not retain an infinite executing turn.\n\n3. Complete the declared-meaning contract without assuming N2 rules \"carry over\" automatically. The current ActType/ItemType tables do not specify opening initial-state and fixed-party initialization, expected revision and immutable terms, required/default values, field/ref schemas, expression inputs, lookup semantics, atomic multi-item effects, or how \"signer\" targets another local commitment item as in witness A.9. Define complete bounded grammar/validation and before/after attention projection, or enumerate each missing form and its R2/R3 owner before claiming D5 answered. Pin the full semantics: definition bytes/digest, opening+transition guards/effects/sends, evaluator/canonicalization profile identity and version, platform capability semantics/version, fixed party initialization and resolved references. A platform string @version is usable only with immutable version meaning and an honest unsupported result. Multiple pinned meanings within the chosen new architecture are normal history, not legacy compatibility; supported first-version meaning evolution and runtime-boundary upgrades need a stated policy. Current roles/keys/grants remain separately current, never inferred from pinned party fields. Application authority cannot be \"any\" alone; role/action grant AND actual party checks must be expressible.\n\n4. Make replay verification and its evidence limits precise. Section 9.3 step 4 must recursively derive or use an explicitly trusted/anchored proof of the foreign source fact under its own genesis, meaning and authority; an entry's content hash matching its claimed hash proves byte consistency, not that the source admitted that duty/report/grant. Include platform Genesis/Delivery/Timed/Outcome/Checkpoint semantics, retained evaluated inputs/results and how guards/effects/sends are rederived for each; specify external-outcome evidence and what replay can check versus what it trusts. A hash chain and actor signatures alone do not attest the service's clock, admit a delivery or settle a real external write. Section 9.4 properly names equivocation as a limit; retain it and distinguish pinned caller-supplied heads/anchored evidence from service-trusting unanchored histories. Bound dependency traversal, nodes/bytes/depth, repeated facts and missing/cyclic/unavailable source histories; keep canonical hash checking before relying on the content. Receipt anchoring in the same Git push is a proposed R3 protocol: state atomic-ref capability, fence and crash/readback questions, no hidden guarantee. \"verified\" must name its mode, exact inputs/heads/coverage, unresolved dependencies and service/provider trust. No earlier-format verifier need survive, but the useful 016 disclosure rule remains.\n\n5. Distinguish retryable unavailability, stable refusal and accepted receipt recovery. Section 4 promises cached exact refusals within an unspecified window; section 12 says dependency-unavailable retries may recover. If the same operation's temporary refusal is permanently cached, those claims conflict. Specify which answer consumes an idempotency key, which may be reevaluated, mismatch scope/key/digest, finite retry window/tombstone expiry and how unavailable data never silently turns an old accepted operation into a new effect. Accepted receipts after revocation may be fetched through a settlement-only path without granting new acts, fresh sessions or fork writes. Refusals need not be semantic entries; if their retry table changes outside history, explain how it is excluded from semantic replay and how affected admission dependencies are checked. In the inbox, classify duplicate accepted deliveries, terminal refused delivery versus retryable inability to decide, late lower relationship revisions, recorded refusal versus no-entry response. A receiver refusal currently returns no entry while section 7.1 says every delivery is recorded; reconcile that. An authoritative page state about a refused child/report or an owner's owed action needs a retained producer/consumer outcome; it cannot depend on transient error text. \"An answer becomes an entry when a page needs it\" is too arbitrary: distinguish authoritative lifecycle outcome from advisory transport bookkeeping and make ownership/durable acknowledgment explicit. Multi-owner relationship keys must include owner scope+incarnation and relationship identity; seq is not comparable between unrelated owners/incarnations.\n\n6. Complete interruption, retention and shared-boundary coverage. Budget exhaustion cannot prevent recording expiry/revocation, cleanup, a confirmed external result or an outstanding unknown duty forever. R4 must specify reserved settlement capacity/backpressure and bounded export/read continuation; name it here as a contract dependency. Retain exact new-model definitions, input/foreign-fact closure and result evidence needed for replay until the corresponding history/effect retention ends; calling a required local definition copy a discardable cache cannot allow the source to disappear without an explicit incomplete result and no claim of full replay. Reset/deletion requires externally settled effects/credentials, not just all current inbox duties marked delivered. Holding all history locally may be the initial policy, but do not imply it makes total replay bounded: each read is paged; whole-run work and scope-full recovery remain R4 obligations. Explain the chosen nonexclusive cross-lane extent behavior as a deliberate product tradeoff and make R2 demonstrate safe independent forks, same-path conflict reporting and no accidental shared physical workspace. A hold still fences its own holder/epoch. No overlap monitor or stale directory copy can be treated as write authority. Sole-admin facts need a current grant/commitment contract under R3 if they affect present review qualification.\n\n7. Fix exact-contribution provenance and inventory claims. Witness 10.2 fills authors from A.9's reporting signer only. A selected contribution can contain work authored by a prior holder U, other selected authors, or delegated principal/signing-key provenance. Specify recorded attribution closure and authenticated member/key/grant relationships that determine independent-review eligibility; a reporting member is not automatically its only author, and Git author text is not authority. The owner-review choice in 005 remains explicit policy disclosure, with the new identity/review contract in R3. Witness10.3 checks issue close.from against the PR merge fact but the owner may issue a later confirmed publication/close-link duty: name the exact source receipt/relationship revision, no self-hash and no closure before confirmed effect. Section 11 should say five packages are mapped, not six; admission inventory's S1 title is historical label for an audit now used by R1. Identifier removal acceptance cannot merely ban generic Seq/Generation names while new item IDs are scope-local seq: acceptance is absence of the old global/lease semantics and correct qualified references. Root/build/export/import/config/test-runner rewrites and active guides still belong to exact R0 successors; useful new-model meaning versions are not a prohibited second architecture.\n\nPlease return one corrected exact draft plus a concise correction matrix before independent review. R2/R3 design may develop against explicit unresolved interfaces; no source implementation starts from these unadopted proposals. Keep composable local admission, exact immutable contributions, truthful missing/unsupported outcomes and explicit removal; do not reinstate a global Room queue or old-format support.","body":{"head":"8416c414df68f11b2a789ebb686de0bf1895c4d4","request":"c69b573d910a2ead7874a8651d022530dc58be85","scope":"Internal planner correction before independent review; not adoption or source approval"}}